Use `terminated` for normal delivery with no app process, and `force-stop` for the distinct Android force-stopped case. A custom trigger cannot use Android `cold`.

```json
{
  "matrix_version": 2,
  "platform": "android",
  "device_id": "emulator-5554",
  "target_kind": "android-emulator",
  "target_label": "android-fcm-test",
  "include_device_id": false,
  "app_id": "com.example.app",
  "timeout_seconds": 60,
  "context": {
    "flavor": "staging",
    "build_mode": "debug",
    "entrypoint": "lib/main_staging.dart",
    "data_source": "FCM test fixture",
    "provider": "Firebase Cloud Messaging"
  },
  "scenarios": [
    {
      "name": "terminated-fcm-tap-normal-delivery",
      "lifecycle": "terminated",
      "before": [
        {
          "name": "prepare signed-in notification fixture",
          "argv": ["tool/test-state", "notification-fixture", "order-42"]
        }
      ],
      "precondition": [
        {
          "name": "verify app process absent and FCM remains deliverable",
          "argv": ["tool/assert-runtime-state", "process-absent", "not-force-stopped", "fcm-registered"]
        }
      ],
      "trigger": {
        "name": "send FCM and tap its notification",
        "argv": ["tool/send-and-tap-fcm", "order-42"]
      },
      "assert": [
        {
          "name": "open order destination once",
          "argv": ["tool/assert-flow", "order-detail", "42"]
        }
      ],
      "after": [
        {
          "name": "clear notification fixture",
          "argv": ["tool/test-state", "clear"]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "force-stop-fcm-tap",
      "lifecycle": "force-stop",
      "before": [
        {
          "name": "prepare signed-in notification fixture",
          "argv": ["tool/test-state", "notification-fixture", "order-42"]
        }
      ],
      "trigger": {
        "name": "send FCM and tap its notification",
        "argv": ["tool/send-and-tap-fcm", "order-42"]
      },
      "assert": [
        {
          "name": "verify expected post-force-stop behavior",
          "argv": ["tool/assert-force-stop-fcm-behavior", "order-42"]
        }
      ],
      "after": [
        {
          "name": "clear notification fixture",
          "argv": ["tool/test-state", "clear"]
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
```

Key distinction:

- `terminated` does not make the runner stop the process. Its precondition must prove the app is absent while the package is not force-stopped and FCM registration/delivery remains valid.
- `force-stop` explicitly runs Android force-stop before the trigger. Account for Android’s FCM behavior after force-stop—delivery may not resume until the user reopens the app—so the assertion should encode the product’s intended behavior rather than assume a notification appears.
- Both rows should retain idempotent `after` cleanup; the runner calls it even when a prior hook fails or times out. Run the matrix dry run first, verify its `planned` result, then execute.
